content.thinking:
Wait — there's a subtlety with the queue: "the queue admitting the fourth toast only after one expires". With my design, when the oldest visible toast expires, the queued 4th toast becomes visible — but it never got its timer scheduled (scheduleDismiss was only called on push for currently-visible ones). So after an expiry, the newly promoted toast would never auto-dismiss. I need to schedule timers for promoted toasts when visibility changes.

Better approach: use a single effect that watches `toasts` and manages timers for all visible toasts. But hover-pausing mutates entries. Let me restructure: keep an effect that runs whenever `toasts` changes, and for each visible toast with resumedAt !== null, ensure a timer exists; clear timers for non-visible or paused ones.

Let me rewrite more cleanly:

- state: toasts array
- effect on [toasts]: compute visible = last 3. For each entry in visible:
  - if resumedAt !== null and no timer exists → set timer for remainingMs
  - if resumedAt === null → ensure no timer
  - For entries not visible → ensure no timer (they're paused implicitly? but their remainingMs should still count... they're queued, countdown shouldn't start until visible). When pushed, resumedAt = now() only if it will be visible. Simpler: on push, set resumedAt = now(); effect handles scheduling only for visible; queued entries have timer running conceptually... Actually if a queued toast has resumedAt=now() at push, by the time it becomes visible its remainingMs is stale. Fix: when promoting to visible, reset remaining based on elapsed? Simplest correct semantics: queued toasts don't count down until visible. So on push, if it lands beyond visible window, set resumedAt = null (paused) so countdown starts when promoted.

Effect approach:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const visible = toasts.slice(-MAX_VISIBLE_TOASTS);
  const visibleIds = new Set(visible.map(t => t.id));
  // clear timers not needed
  for (const [id, handle] of timers.current) {
    const t = toasts.find(x => x.id === id);
    if (!t || !visibleIds.has(id) || t.resumedAt === null) {
      clearTimeout(handle);
      timers.current.delete(id);
    }
  }
  // schedule
  for (const entry of visible) {
    if (entry.resumedAt === null) continue;
    if (timers.current.has(entry.id)) continue;
    const id = entry.id;
    const handle = setTimeout(() => expire(id), entry.remainingMs);
    timers.current.set(id, handle);
  }
}, [toasts]);
```

expire removes from state; effect cleanup clears timers.

But pause/resume mutate state which retriggers effect — fine. Pause computes remaining from Date.now() - resumedAt; resume sets resumedAt = now(), effect schedules new timer with remainingMs.

One catch with fake timers + Date.now(): vi.useFakeTimers() by default fakes Date too, so Date.now() advances with advanceTimersByTime. Good.

Hover pause test: fire mouseOver on toast, advance past duration, assert present, mouseOut, advance, gone. With my implementation: mouseOver → pause sets resumedAt=null, clears timer. Effect reruns, doesn't schedule since resumedAt null. Good. mouseOut → resume sets resumedAt=now, effect schedules timer with remainingMs. Good.

Queue promotion: push 4 toasts quickly. Toasts 1-3 visible with timers; toast 4 queued, resumedAt should be... If I set resumedAt=now() on push regardless, then when toast 1 expires, toast 4 promotes; its remainingMs hasn't been decremented, and the effect schedules a fresh timer for remainingMs (full duration). Since resumedAt was stale, remainingMs is still full duration because we never recomputed. That's actually fine — countdown effectively starts at promotion. But cleaner: on push, mark queued ones resumedAt = now() anyway; effect only schedules visible. remainingMs stays full until first pause. OK, semantics work.

Actually simpler: set resumedAt = now() always on push. Effect handles everything. Let me rewrite the component.

