Now update the queue test to match FIFO semantics:
- after 4 pushes: visible = [toast-1, toast-2, toast-3], last is toast-3 (newest-last within stack).
- After DURATION: toast-1 expires → visible [2,3,4].
- After another DURATION -1 +1: toast-2 expires (it started at t=0) → [3,4]... wait, toast-4 started when promoted (at DURATION), so it dismisses at 2*DURATION. toast-3 started at 0, dismisses at DURATION too? No — all of toasts 1-3 started at push time t≈0 with duration 3000. At advance(DURATION): timers for 1, 2, AND 3 all fire! They were scheduled at same time. So after advancing DURATION, toasts 1,2,3 all expire and toast-4 promotes. Hmm — in my earlier test expectation I had them expire one by one, which was wrong even before... Actually with fake timers, setTimeout(3000) scheduled at the same tick all fire at advanceTimersByTime(3000). So all three expire simultaneously. That's realistic.

But then "queue admitting fourth only after one expires" — advancing past duration removes all three and admits the 4th. To test admission-after-expiry more precisely, push toasts with staggered remaining times? Simpler: push first toast, advance some time so its deadline is earliest, then push others. Or use different durations per toast. Let's do: push toast A with duration 1000, then B, C, D with default 3000. Visible: A,B,C; D queued. Advance 1000 → A expires → D admitted. Assert D present while B,C still present, and that before advancing, D was not present.

Also assert countdown doesn't run while queued: hover-free B/C expire at their own time; check D still present at 1000 but B,C gone at 3000 from their start, and D goes at 1000+3000.

Let me restructure queue test:

```tsx
function MultiHarness() {
  const { push } = useToast();
  return (
    <button onClick={() => {
      push(`toast-${pushCount}`, { durationMs: pushCount === 1 ? 1000 : DURATION });
      pushCount++;
    }}>push</button>
  );
}
```

Simpler to inline in the test rather than Harness. Let me rewrite that test:

```tsx
it("queues the fourth toast until one expires", () => {
  let n = 0;
  function QueueHarness() {
    const { push } = useToast();
    return (
      <button onClick={() => {
        n++;
        push(`toast-${n}`, { durationMs: n === 1 ? 1000 : DURATION });
      }}>push</button>
    );
  }
  render(<ToastProvider><QueueHarness /></ToastProvider>);
  for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) fireEvent.click(screen.getByText("push"));

  const texts = () => getToasts().map(t => t.textContent!.replace("Dismiss", ""));
  expect(texts()).toEqual(["toast-1", "toast-2", "toast-3"]);

  // Queued toast does not count down: at t=999 nothing expired yet.
  act(() => { vi.advanceTimersByTime(999); });
  expect(getToasts()).toHaveLength(3);

  act(() => { vi.advanceTimersByTime(1); }); // t=1000: oldest expires
  expect(texts()).toEqual(["toast-2", "toast-3", "toast-4"]); // fourth admitted

  // Fourth counts its own full duration from promotion.
  act(() => { vi.advanceTimersByTime(DURATION - 1); });
  expect(getToasts()).toHaveLength(3);
  act(() => { vi.advanceTimersByTime(1); });
  expect(getToasts().map(t => t.textContent)).toEqual(["toast-2Dismiss","toast-3Dismiss"... 
